// Second-pass omission audit. Fresh attention on the same chunk beats a same-call
// self-audit, which models rubber-stamp. Framed as "find omissions" only — it never
// rewrites or re-tiers what the first pass already produced.
const KB_VERIFY_PROMPT=`You are auditing a completed extraction for OMISSIONS ONLY.

You are given (A) a source chunk and (B) the list of facts already extracted from it.
Your only job is to find substantive clinical content present in the source chunk that is NOT represented in the extracted list.

Return valid JSON only. Do not wrap the JSON in a markdown fence.

JSON SHAPE:
{"missed":[{
  "conditionName":"string — the condition/topic this fact belongs to, matching the source's own naming",
  "text":"string",
  "latteBucket":"BriefPatho|Look|Assess|Tests|Treatments|Educate",
  "subtype":"early|late|independent|dependent|acute|discharge|null",
  "factType":"mechanism|symptom|assessment|test|medication|intervention|education|threshold|contraindication|other",
  "safetyCritical":false,
  "tier":1,
  "sourceQuote":"verbatim source phrase, 20 words maximum"
}]}

REPORT AS MISSED:
- a drug, dose, unit, route, frequency, or timing not represented
- a numeric value, lab range, or vital-sign threshold not represented
- a hold parameter, notify threshold, contraindication, or precaution not represented
- a sign, symptom, red flag, or deterioration finding not represented
- a nursing assessment or intervention not represented
- a teaching point or when-to-seek-care instruction not represented
- a qualifier that changes meaning and was dropped: population, exception, timing, route, or a comparison word such as increased, decreased, above, below

DO NOT REPORT:
- content already represented in the extracted list, even if worded differently
- slide titles, headers, footers, page furniture, citations, learning objectives
- restatements that add no new clinical detail
- anything not physically present in this source chunk

HARD RULES
- NEVER invent. Every returned fact must be supported by text in THIS chunk.
- Every returned fact MUST carry a verbatim sourceQuote of 20 words or fewer, copied character-for-character from the chunk. A fact whose quote cannot be located in the chunk WILL BE DISCARDED automatically.
- Do not add outside clinical knowledge, even when it is correct and relevant.
- Finding nothing is a valid and expected result. If nothing substantive was missed, return {"missed":[]}.
- Do NOT pad this list to appear thorough. An empty result is better than a fabricated one. You are not scored on how many items you return.
- Assign latteBucket, subtype, factType, safetyCritical, and tier using the same rules the original extraction used.`;

// Focus context for the Knowledge Base. Two modes, because "tailor this" means two
// incompatible things at the extraction layer:
//   prioritize — extract everything, demote out-of-scope content to Tier 3 (non-destructive)
//   scope      — skip out-of-scope content entirely (smaller/cheaper, but lossy and permanent)
// The same block is appended to BOTH passes; otherwise the omission audit would spend its
// effort "recovering" exactly the content the scope filter was told to drop.
function kbBuildFocusBlock(outcomes,points,extra,mode){
  const seg=[];
  if(String(outcomes||'').trim())seg.push('OUTCOMES (learning objectives this exam covers):\n'+outcomes.trim());
  if(String(points||'').trim())seg.push('POINTS (specific content that must be covered):\n'+points.trim());
  if(String(extra||'').trim())seg.push('ADDITIONAL CONTEXT:\n'+extra.trim());
  if(!seg.length)return'';
  const rules=mode==='scope'?
`HOW TO APPLY THIS FOCUS CONTEXT — SCOPE MODE (deliberately restrictive):
- Extract ONLY content that supports a listed outcome or point, plus the background a student needs to understand it.
- Content in this chunk that is clearly unrelated to every listed outcome and point should be SKIPPED. Skipping it is correct and expected in this mode, and does not violate the zero-skip rules above.
- If this chunk contains no in-scope content at all, return {"conditions":[]}. An empty result is a valid answer here.
- Within in-scope content, every completeness rule above still applies at FULL strength: every numeric value, dose, route, threshold, contraindication, and teaching point.
- When you cannot tell whether something is in scope, INCLUDE it. Ambiguity always resolves toward inclusion.`
:
`HOW TO APPLY THIS FOCUS CONTEXT — PRIORITIZE MODE (nothing is skipped):
- Extract the source COMPLETELY. Do NOT omit content because it falls outside the focus context. Every zero-skip rule above still applies in full.
- Use the focus context ONLY to steer tier assignment:
  - Content matching a listed outcome or point is Tier 1.
  - Content related to the focus but not directly named keeps its normal tier.
  - Content clearly unrelated to every listed outcome and point is Tier 3.
- Clearly out-of-scope content is Tier 3 even when it is a drug, dose, threshold, or safety warning. This deliberately overrides the "always Tier 1" tier rules above, and applies ONLY to content unrelated to every listed outcome and point.
- Every listed outcome and point that this source actually covers must be represented by at least one extracted fact.`;
  return'\n\n═══ STUDENT-PROVIDED FOCUS CONTEXT ═══\n'+seg.join('\n\n')+'\n\n'+rules+'\n═══════════════════════════════════════\n';
}

// Underextraction flag: relative density (facts per 1000 chars) against the run's own
// median, plus absolute floors. Diagnostics only — never added to the knowledge base.
function kbFlagUnderextraction(stats,scoped){
  const valid=stats.filter(s=>!s.failed&&s.chars>=1500);
  const dens=valid.map(s=>s.facts/(s.chars/1000)).sort((a,b)=>a-b);
  const med=dens.length?dens[Math.floor(dens.length/2)]:0;
  return stats.map(s=>{
    const density=s.chars?s.facts/(s.chars/1000):0;
    let flag=null;
    if(s.failed)flag='failed';
    // In scope mode a thin or empty chunk is the filter working, not a defect. Density
    // comparisons are meaningless once output is deliberately restricted, so only the
    // empty case is surfaced — and as a "confirm this" prompt, not a warning.
    else if(scoped)flag=(s.chars>=1500&&s.facts===0)?'scoped-zero':null;
    else if(s.chars>=1500&&s.facts===0)flag='zero';
    else if(s.chars>=1500&&density<0.25)flag='sparse';
    else if(valid.length>=3&&med>0&&s.chars>=1500&&density<med*0.5)flag='below-median';
